\section{One Printer Can Have Many Valid Inverses}

A disassembler printer picks a string for an instruction. An assembly parser has a different contract: it must accept the supported spellings, report operand errors, and recover the instruction with enough information to emit it. Spaces and register aliases may vary even when the same bytes result. Conversely, if the printer omits a field that can have multiple values, parsing its output cannot recover all original words unless that field is determined by other operands. In the February 2024 VADL preprint, arXiv:2402.09087v1, Sections~3.3 and~3.7 deliberately separate canonical ISA-level formatting from an auxiliary assembly grammar \cite{himmelbauer2024vadl}.

The ISA \texttt{assembly} clause is a string expression; a comma inside the parenthesized expression concatenates its results. A string literal such as \verb|", "| contributes actual punctuation. Built-ins like \texttt{mnemonic}, \texttt{register}, and \texttt{decimal} describe how to render parts of an instruction. This suffices for a canonical spelling without requiring the ISA author to enumerate every accepted spacing or alternative. A separate \texttt{assembly description} can provide grammar rules, aliases for directives, and names for modifiers. The detailed Section~3.7 example associates the description with an ABI, so its register names can use that ABI's assignments.

\section{Read an Explicit Rule as Typed Extraction}

This is the rule from Listing~24, shown alone and with spacing normalized:

\begin{verbatim}
LuiInstruction : (
  mnemonic="lui" @operand
  rd=<RegisterOperand>
  ","
  imm20=<ImmediateOperand>
) @instruction
\end{verbatim}

The rule records a mnemonic and two named operands; it requires a comma rather than storing one. The two operand nonterminals have built-in definitions that can be overridden. For example, accepting a token as a register needs more than identifying a lexical word: a conversion must check that the name denotes a supported register. After parsing, the Section~4.5.2 prototype matches on the named operands and their contents. Satisfying a grammar alternative alone does not prove that a legal instruction instance exists. An immediate may parse as an integer and still exceed its range; a symbol with a modifier must retain information for later resolution.

