\section{Infer the Structure, Not a List of Every Line}

Sections~4.5.2 and~4.5.5 describe two paths to a rule. The hybrid inference algorithm examines VIR operations for structural cases such as concatenation; it falls back to interpretation when it encounters operations it does not directly handle. Interpretation can tabulate outputs for small sets of inputs, but one twenty-bit field has \(2^{20}\) values before considering any other operand. A table of full strings also throws away distinctions useful for error messages. This teaching trace illustrates a structural inversion and the point where a hand-written rule can take over; it is not VADL grammar syntax:

\begin{verbatim}
printer pieces: literal("lui "), register(rd),
                literal(", "), render(imm20)
structural result: "lui" REGISTER-as-rd "," IMMEDIATE-as-imm20
  # Use terminal and typed nonterminal rules, not exact spaces.
parse "lui ra, 12":
  mnemonic="lui", rd=1, imm20=12   # with the example ABI
  find the corresponding instruction, then check its range
parse "lui nowhere, 12":
  fail register conversion       # a word is not always a register
\end{verbatim}

With symbolic input such as \verb|lui ra, %hi(main)|, immediate conversion must carry \texttt{main} and the modifier forward, not manufacture a numeric constant. \Cref{fig:visual-326} contrasts this shared internal form with the one canonical spelling a printer elects to return.

