\section{Review Printer Edits as Grammar Edits}

If an architect omits a default operand from the pretty printer, the inferred parser may change even though no encoding or behavior changes. Test that both long and abbreviated forms yield the intended operands, that leading tokens do not create an unresolved parser choice, and that the printer's result still parses. When an omitted operand is not uniquely determined, either define a canonical choice or retain enough text to distinguish it. Rule factoring can keep common prefixes from consuming lookahead; it is a grammar optimization, not a reason to loosen operand checks.

The generated parser's useful boundary is therefore explicit:

\begin{verbatim}
tokens   = lex(source)
operands = parse_LuiInstruction(tokens)  # grammar and typed conversion
match    = find_instruction("lui", operands)
require immediate_is_encodable(match, operands.imm20)
emit_internal_instruction(match, operands)
\end{verbatim}

Memoizing a typed conversion or factoring a common prefix can improve parser generation and runtime, but neither can replace the final instruction match and format-range check. In particular, a relocation expression may be syntactically an immediate while remaining deliberately non-numeric until object generation.

Compare diagnostics before and after that change, too. For example, a missing comma and a register whose spelling is unknown should not both be reported as an unknown mnemonic. The structure inferred from a concatenation gives the parser a place to report each error.

The Section~5.5 prototype used LL(1) recursive descent and reused LLVM tokenization with a built-in terminal set. Its inference counts were 74 of 74 instructions for the RV32I specification tested, 104 of 106 for MIPS IV, and 1437 of 1439 for AArch64. The paper reports trouble with MIPS abbreviations and with conveying some AArch64 syntactic distinctions to the instruction matcher. Counts of inferred rules are evidence about those formatting functions, not proof that every legal spelling is recognized or that the same limits apply to any later implementation.
